<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narwar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narwar is a town in the Shivpuri district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> river, below a large hilltop fort. Associated in legend with King Nala of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>, it was held by a branch of the Kachchhapaghatas and later by the Jajapellas, Tomaras, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachhwahas</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Narwar lies at an average elevation of 452 metres, 42 km from Shivpuri. Its fort stands just east of the Sindh River and is surrounded by the Kali Sindh. Three dams lie near the town: the Harsi Dam, Mohini Sagar and the Madikheda dam. Many medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscriptions name the place Nalpura, the town of Nala.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Narwar is identified with Nalapura, the town of the Nishad Charita written by Shriharsha, capital of Nala, whose love for Damayanti is told at length in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a>. In the legend Nala left Damayanti asleep in the forests of Narwar, and she made her way through the dense forest to Chanderi, protecting herself from wild animals.</p><p>On 12 August 1602 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak">Abu'l-Fazl ibn Mubarak</a>, the courtier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> who wrote the Akbarnama, was killed near Narwar on his way back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. The attack, made by Vir Singh Bundela between Sarai Vir and Antri, was a plot contrived by Akbar's eldest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Prince Salim</a>, the later Emperor Jahangir, whose accession Abu'l-Fazl was known to oppose. His severed head was sent to Salim at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a>, and he was buried at Antri. In 1707 the Mughal emperor Bahadur Shah I made Maharaja Gopal Singh of the Bhadawar state the imperial governor of Narwar.</p><p>In January 1859 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatya-tope">Tatya Tope</a>, who had escaped alone into the jungles of Paron after his defeat by the British, found refuge with Man Singh Kachwaha, Raja of Narwar, then in dispute with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Gwalior. The British promised Man Singh his safety and protection from the Maharaja's reprisals, and he led a detachment of General Richard John Meade's troops to Tatya Tope's camp, where he was captured asleep on 7 April 1859 and taken to Shivpuri for trial by a military court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Narwar is a nagar parishad of the Shivpuri district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>; in the Gwalior State it had been the seat of Narwar District. At the 2001 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> it had 15,748 inhabitants.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Narwar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Kachchhapaghatas emerged in the 10th century in the region of Gwalior, <b>Narwar</b> and Dubkund in northern Madhya Pradesh, probably as feudatories of the Gurjara-Pratihara or the Chandela dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/kachchhapaghata-dynasty">Kachchhapaghata dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
